  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q34-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "The sum of thirteen positive integers is 1000. What is the smallest possible value of their LCM?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p19-q34-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "80",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p19-q34-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Exclude LCM values below 80; twelve copies of 80 and one 40 attain the required sum.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $L$ be the LCM. Each of the thirteen integers divides $L$, so each is at most $L$. Since $13\\times76=988<1000$, we need $L\\ge77$.\n\nIf $L=77$, its divisors are $1,7,11,77$. Thirteen copies of 77 sum to $1001$. Replacing any 77 by a smaller divisor reduces the sum by at least $77-11=66$, so a sum of 1000 is impossible.\n\nIf $L=78$, its divisors are $1,2,3,6,13,26,39,78$. Thirteen copies of 78 sum to $1014$. Replacing any 78 by a smaller divisor reduces the sum by at least $78-39=39$, so a sum of 1000 is impossible.\n\nIf $L=79$, its only divisors are $1,79$. Thirteen copies of 79 sum to $1027$. Any replacement reduces the sum by 78, so a sum of 1000 is impossible.\n\nFor $L=80$, take twelve integers equal to 80 and one equal to 40:\n$$12\\times80+40=1000.$$\nTheir LCM is 80. Therefore, the smallest possible LCM is $80$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
